CONTENT
-------

This distribution includes the source of Yices, documentation,
tests, and examples.

Yices 2 is developed by Bruno Dutertre and Dejan Jovanovic, at the
Computer Science Laboratory, SRI International. To contact us, report
a bug, or to get more information about Yices, please visit our
website at https://yices.csl.sri.com.


PREREQUISITES
-------------

To build Yices from the source, you need:

- GCC version 4.0.x or newer (or clang 3.0 or newer)
- gperf version 3.0 or newer
- the GMP library version 4.1 or newer

+ other standard tools: make (gnumake is required), sed, etc.


To build the manual, you also need:

- a latex installation
- the latexmk tool


QUICK INSTALLATION
------------------

Do this:

  ./configure
  make
  sudo make install

This will install binaries and libraries in /usr/local/. It also
installs LICENSE and NOTICES in /usr/local/share/doc/yices-2.8.0,
and the license texts in /usr/local/share/licenses/yices-2.8.0.
You can change the installation location by giving option 
  --prefix=... to the ./configure script.

For more explanations, please check doc/COMPILING.


SOURCE REVISION
---------------

The official release binaries record the full release commit. The release
workflow supplies it through YICES_SOURCE_REVISION.

A normal build from this extracted source archive has no Git metadata. It
therefore reports "Revision: unknown" unless YICES_SOURCE_REVISION is set.
This is intentional: a locally modified source tree must not silently claim
to be the pristine release commit. For a byte-unmodified official source
archive, you may set YICES_SOURCE_REVISION to the full commit recorded in the
published YICES-SOURCE-RECEIPT before running make. Keep a separate record of
any local modifications.

Generated src/api/yices_*_version.c files are intentionally omitted from this
source archive. The build regenerates the appropriate version source from
the configured mode and YICES_SOURCE_REVISION (or bounded Git discovery).
The source receipt binds the archived inputs, not a previous builder's
transient version file.



SUPPORT FOR NON-LINEAR ARITHMETIC
---------------------------------

This release has experimental support for non-linear real arithmetic,
but it is not enabled by default. If you want non-linear arithmetic,
follow these instructions:

1) Install SRI's LibPoly and CUDD, including their headers and libraries.
   The release uses LibPoly 0.2.1 at c342ff1ad2f0cbcfc866b1ef024003ccc001add2
   (https://github.com/SRI-CSL/libpoly) and CUDD 3.0.0 at
   f54f533303640afd5dbe47a05ebeabb3066f2a25 (https://github.com/ivmai/cudd).
   Their exact corresponding-source archives accompany the release. Follow
   their build instructions; use PIC dependencies for the Yices shared
   library (CUDD: configure CFLAGS=-fPIC; LibPoly: enable its static PIC build).
   These are tested identities, not claimed minimum compatible versions.

2) After you've installed LibPoly and CUDD, configure Yices with the
   flag '--enable-mcsat', then build Yices as usual:

    ./configure --enable-mcsat
    make
    sudo make install

   You may need to provide LDFLAGS/CPPFLAGS if ./configure fails to
   find either library and its headers. Other options may be useful too. Try
   ./configure --help to see what's there.


TEST COVERAGE AND OPTIONAL TOOLS
-------------------------------

The source archive includes API/unit tests and the arrays, bv, efsmt, mantis
and wd regression groups. The test targets have different purposes:

- make check builds the solvers and runs the included regression groups.
  For a static build, use make static-check.
- make check-api builds and runs the API tests. For a static build, use
  make static-check-api.
- make test builds the unit-test programs; it does not execute them.
  make static-test builds their static counterparts. Some programs require
  arguments or input files: consult their source before running them
  individually from build/<target>-<mode>/bin (or static_bin).

On Linux, static-check-api and static-test also build the shared library with
static GMP. If the system libgmp.a is not PIC, supply a PIC archive through
configure's --with-pic-gmp option (and its matching header if needed); see
doc/COMPILING. A non-PIC system archive alone is not sufficient for those
targets.

With Python 3 installed, python3 tests/unit/test_regress_harness.py checks the
regression driver's own failure accounting and path handling, without building
Yices. It uses controlled solver/parallel-dispatch fixtures; it is not a
solver-correctness suite or a substitute for the targets above.

The larger MCSAT regression suite remains in the Git repository and
is not included in this archive. Requesting make mcsat-check or
make static-mcsat-regress without that suite fails; zero tests is not a PASS.
For that coverage use a matching Git checkout with the suite and an
MCSAT-enabled build. The release build's recorded MCSAT results do not mean
that a recipient ran those tests.

The optional Python 3 portfolio helper utils/yices2_parallel.py also remains
in the Git repository rather than these source/binary archives. The manual's
portfolio examples require that checkout and an explicit solver path.



WINDOWS BUILD
-------------

We recommend compiling using Cygwin. If you want a version that works
natively on Windows (i.e., does not depend on the Cygwin DLLs), you
can compile from Cygwin using the MinGW cross-compilers. This is
explained in doc/COMPILING.


DOCUMENTATION
-------------

To build the manual from the source, type

   make doc

This will build ./doc/manual/manual.pdf.

Other documentation is in the ./doc directory:

- doc/COMPILING explains the compilation process and options in detail.
- doc/NOTES gives an overview of the source code.
- doc/YICES-LANGUAGE explains the syntax of the Yices language, and
  describes commands, functions, and heuristic parameters.
